<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0080116> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Every potential in physics is the same mathematical object — a partial derivative of energy with respect to its conjugate extensive quantity. The differences are only in which extensive quality you're considering."@en .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0080116> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "potential"@en .
<https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0080116> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "a partial derivative of energy with respect to its conjugate extensive quantity"@en .
_:genid1389 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Axiom> .
_:genid1389 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#annotatedSource> <https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0080116> .
_:genid1389 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#annotatedProperty> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> .
_:genid1389 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#annotatedTarget> _:genid1384 .
_:genid1389 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "In field theories (e.g., gravity, electrostatics), the potential becomes a field ϕ(r⃗), and the force is recovered via:\nF=−∇ϕ(r⃗)F\nThis is the spatial analogue: instead of asking \"how does energy change per unit carrier added?\", you ask \"how does energy change per unit carrier moved in space?\""@en .
